# Scoped API Tokens and Service Accounts

The provider accepts three kinds of credentials:

| Credentials | `email_address` | `token` | `auth_type` |
|---|---|---|---|
| User with a classic API token | the user's email | classic (unscoped) API token | `classic` (default) |
| User with a scoped API token | the user's email | scoped API token | `scoped` |
| Service account | `...@serviceaccount.atlassian.com` | the service account's scoped API token | `scoped` (the default for this address) |

Classic API tokens keep working exactly as before; nothing in existing configurations needs to change.

~> **Note:** scoped API tokens and service accounts are supported on Jira Service Management sites only
(`product_type = "jira-service-desk"`). The Compass Operations API accepts basic authentication with classic API
tokens only and defines no scopes, so with `product_type = "compass"` use a classic API token. On Compass, the
`atlassian-operations_user` data source cannot find a service account by email; use the service account's account
ID (admin.atlassian.com → Directory → Service accounts) directly where it is needed, for example in a team's
`member` set.

## Prerequisites

The account running Terraform (the user or service account `token` belongs to) needs Jira Service Management
product access, granted in Atlassian Administration (admin.atlassian.com → Directory → Users or Service accounts);
without it every call fails with a `403` saying the account does not have access to Operations. To
create a team and its schedules, policies, integrations and heartbeats in one apply, make the account running
Terraform a JSM Operations product admin. Without that, the team's Operations admin is the member whose account ID
sorts first as text (the provider picks it), and when that is not the
account running Terraform, the team is created but its schedules, policies, integrations and heartbeats fail with
`403` "You are not authorized …". A team admin can then make the account an Operations admin of that team in the
Operations UI, and the next apply creates the rest. Service accounts are not affected: a service account creates
the team and all of its Operations resources without being the nominee.

Global maintenance windows (`atlassian-operations_maintenance` without `team_id`) additionally need a JSM Operations
product admin; a team's Operations admin alone gets a `403` saying it is not authorized to update maintenance.
Service accounts are not affected.

## Create the tokens

**A user's two scoped API tokens.** Go to id.atlassian.com → Security → API tokens → **Create API token with
scopes** ([Manage API tokens for your Atlassian account](https://support.atlassian.com/atlassian-account/docs/manage-api-tokens-for-your-atlassian-account/)). Give it a name and an expiry (1-365 days), select the app for
the scopes below, select the scopes, Create, and copy the token: it cannot be shown again. The app is chosen per
token, so one token cannot hold the Teams scopes together with the Jira/JSM scopes; a user therefore creates two
tokens (a service account token can carry both scope lists and needs only one; see below):

* `token`: select the **Jira** app, with the scopes `read:jira-user`, `read:ops-config:jira-service-management`,
  `write:ops-config:jira-service-management` and `delete:ops-config:jira-service-management`.
* `teams_token`: select the **Teams** app, with the scopes `view:team:teams`, `view:membership:teams`,
  `write:team:teams`, `write:membership:teams` and `read:account`.

**A service account's one token.** Go to admin.atlassian.com → Directory → Service accounts → the account → API
tokens ([Manage API tokens for service accounts](https://support.atlassian.com/user-management/docs/manage-api-tokens-for-service-accounts/)). Create a single token with every scope above; a service account does not need
`teams_token`. Use the account's `...@serviceaccount.atlassian.com` address as `email_address` (`auth_type` then
defaults to `scoped`), and add the account to a group with the Jira **Browse users and groups** global permission
for user lookups.

~> **Note:** scoped tokens expire (1-365 days). An expired token shows up as a `401` on api.atlassian.com (see
Troubleshooting below); rotate both tokens together in CI before they expire.

## Enabling a scoped API token

Set `auth_type = "scoped"` in the provider block, or set the `ATLASSIAN_OPS_AUTH_TYPE=scoped` environment variable (the environment variable wins). A service account, that is an `email_address` ending in `@serviceaccount.atlassian.com`, gets `scoped` by default, since service accounts have no classic API token; an explicit value always wins over the default.

