~> **Important:** a service account's own `account_id` must also be listed in a team's `member` set, or the
provider removes it from the team once creation finishes; without membership it can no longer update or delete the
team, so every later `update` or `destroy` of that `atlassian-operations_team` resource then fails. Find a service
account's account ID at admin.atlassian.com, under Directory > Service accounts.

The rest of the configuration stays the same. Resources keep the same IDs and import formats, so an existing state also
keeps working after switching an existing configuration from a classic to a scoped API token.

## What changes with a scoped API token

With `auth_type = "scoped"` the provider calls api.atlassian.com instead of your site domain for teams (the
`atlassian-operations_team` resource and data source) and for user lookups (the `atlassian-operations_user` data
source); add api.atlassian.com to network allowlists.

All other resources keep the API they use today.

~> **Note:** the Teams GraphQL API used for teams with scoped tokens is currently labelled beta by Atlassian.

~> **Note:** creating a team with a scoped token is normally as fast as with a classic token; in the worst case
the provider waits up to about two minutes for Operations to see the new team, so do not cancel the apply.

## Required token scopes

A **user's** scoped API tokens are created per product, and one token cannot hold the Teams scopes together with the
JSM Operations scopes. A user with a scoped token therefore creates **two** tokens:

* `token` (or `ATLASSIAN_OPS_API_TOKEN`): the Jira and JSM Operations scopes, used for everything except teams.
* `teams_token` (or `ATLASSIAN_OPS_API_TEAMS_TOKEN`): the Teams scopes, used only by the `atlassian-operations_team`
  resource and data source. When it is not set, `token` is used for teams as well.

A **service account's** scoped API token can carry all of these scopes at once, so a service account needs only
`token`; `teams_token` stays unset. Classic API tokens never need it.

| Scope | Token (user) | Needed for |
|---|---|---|
| `read:jira-user` | `token` | Looking up users by email (`atlassian-operations_user` data source); the account also needs the Jira **Browse users and groups** global permission, see below |
| `read:ops-config:jira-service-management` | `token` | Reading JSM Operations configuration: schedules, rotations, escalations, routing rules, integrations, policies, heartbeats, maintenance windows |
| `write:ops-config:jira-service-management` | `token` | Creating and updating that configuration, and enabling Operations for a new team |
| `delete:ops-config:jira-service-management` | `token` | Deleting that configuration, including the defaults removed by `delete_default_resources` |
| `view:team:teams` | `teams_token` | Reading teams (`atlassian-operations_team` resource and data source) |
| `view:membership:teams` | `teams_token` | Reading team members |
| `write:team:teams` | `teams_token` | Creating, updating and deleting teams |
| `write:membership:teams` | `teams_token` | Adding and removing team members |
| `read:account` | `teams_token` | The Teams token must carry `read:account`; without it the member list cannot be read |

~> **Note:** For user lookups the scope alone is not enough. The account the token belongs to also needs the
**Browse users and groups** [global permission](https://support.atlassian.com/jira-cloud-administration/docs/manage-global-permissions/) in Jira, as documented for the
[Find users API](https://developer.atlassian.com/cloud/jira/platform/rest/v3/api-group-user-search/#api-rest-api-3-user-search-get). Users usually have it through their default groups; a service account
must be added to a group that has it. Without the permission the API returns **empty results instead of an error**,
so the `atlassian-operations_user` data source fails with "Unable to read user, got an empty response".

## Service accounts

A service account authenticates with its email address (`...@serviceaccount.atlassian.com`) and one scoped API token
created for it that carries all the scopes above (service accounts do not need `teams_token`). The provider authenticates a service account with basic auth only; the service account OAuth 2.0 flow is not supported.

* **Team permissions come from membership.** A service account that is a member of a team can update it, add and
  remove members, and delete it; a service account that is not a member can only view it. After creating a team
  the provider makes the members match `member` and removes the creator when it is not listed, so list the service
  account's `account_id` in `member`, together with at least one human member, whenever Terraform must update the
  team's members or delete the team later; a failed creation is cleaned up automatically. Without it, a later
  `update` fails with `Unable to update team, got error: Teams GraphQL API UpdateTeam errors: We can't find the
  member or team in your organization.`, and `destroy` fails with the same text for `DeleteTeam`; the message does
  not mention permissions, and state shows every `user_permissions` flag as `false`. To recover, have a human team
  admin re-add the service account to the team in the Teams UI, add its `account_id` back to `member`, and apply
  again. If `destroy` itself failed instead, a team admin's classic token can delete the team directly.
* **Reading users.** To look up users with the `atlassian-operations_user` data source, the service account needs the
  **Browse users and groups** [global permission](https://support.atlassian.com/jira-cloud-administration/docs/manage-global-permissions/) in Jira (add it to a group that has the
  permission); see the [Find users API](https://developer.atlassian.com/cloud/jira/platform/rest/v3/api-group-user-search/#api-rest-api-3-user-search-get). Scoped tokens must also call the Jira API through
  `api.atlassian.com`, which the provider does for you (see [401 Unauthorized error when a service account accesses
  the Jira or Confluence API](https://support.atlassian.com/atlassian-cloud/kb/401-unauthorized-error-when-service-account-accesses-jira-or-confluence-api/)).
* **A human Operations admin.** The provider nominates the member whose account ID sorts first as text as the
  team's Operations admin; include at least one human member, who can administer the team in the Operations UI.
  Change the admin in the Operations UI afterwards if needed.
* **No on-call duty.** A service account cannot be a schedule rotation participant. Keep service accounts out of
  `participants`, otherwise the rotation fails with "A participant added to this rotation no longer exists".
* **Team types.** `OPEN` and `MEMBER_INVITE` are the values for teams created with this resource, with every token. `EXTERNAL` teams are linked to an external source and cannot be created through the API; an existing one can be imported and kept with `team_type = "EXTERNAL"`.

A team managed with a service account's token, listing the service account itself alongside a human member:

```terraform
resource "atlassian-operations_team" "example" {
  organization_id = "xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx"
  description     = "This is a team created by Terraform"
  display_name    = "Terraform Team"
  team_type       = "MEMBER_INVITE"
  member = [
    { account_id = "712020:xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx" }, # the service account itself
    { account_id = "70121:xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx" },  # a human member who can administer the team in the Operations UI
  ]
}
```

Not supported with service accounts:

* `atlassian-operations_user_contact`
* `atlassian-operations_custom_role`
* `atlassian-operations_notification_rule`

## Differences in team attributes

* **`user_permissions`** reflects what the token's account can do on this team. For a service account, a member
  can update the team, add and remove members, and delete it; a non-member can only view it, so `update_team`,
  `add_members`, `remove_members` and `delete_team` are all `false` (see Service accounts above for why a service
  account must be a member). For a human, the flags follow their own Atlassian permissions, not just membership.
* **`organization_id`** is still required; the provider keeps the configured value in state.
* **IDs and import formats are unchanged.**

## Troubleshooting

| Error | Likely cause |
|---|---|
| `401 Unauthorized; scope does not match` on a team | `auth_type` is not set to `scoped` on a Jira Service Management site; or the token is missing one of the scopes above. The provider appends this guidance to the error |
| `401 Unauthorized; scope does not match` on a schedule, policy or other Operations resource with `product_type = "compass"` | Scoped API tokens and service accounts are not supported on Compass; use a classic API token (see the note at the top) |
| `Unable to create team, got error: Teams GraphQL API CreateTeam errors: This request does not contain the right authorisation scopes to access this field` | One scoped token is used for everything and `teams_token` is empty: "this field" means the token lacks the Teams scopes. Create the Teams token and set `teams_token` (or `ATLASSIAN_OPS_API_TEAMS_TOKEN`); nothing is created by this failed attempt |
| `Unable to read user, authentication failed` (`X-Seraph-LoginReason: AUTHENTICATED_FAILED`) | Jira rejected the credentials: wrong `email_address` or `token`, or a scoped token sent to the site domain because `auth_type` is still `classic` |
| `401` when looking up a user with `auth_type = "scoped"` | A wrong or expired token, a classic token used with `auth_type = "scoped"`, or the token is missing `read:jira-user` |
| `Unable to read user, got an empty response` | The account lacks the Jira **Browse users and groups** global permission (the API returns empty results), the email address does not exist, or the user hides their email through their profile visibility settings |
| `A participant added to this rotation no longer exists` | A service account, or an ID that is not a raw account ID, is in the rotation's `participants` |
| `403` with "Account does not have access to Operations" | The account running Terraform has no JSM Operations access at all; grant the account Jira Service Management product access in Atlassian Administration before running Terraform |
| `403` ("You are not authorized to create the schedule.", "You are not authorized to create policies!", or similar) on a team's schedules, policies, integrations or heartbeats right after it is created | The account running Terraform is not a JSM Operations product admin and is not the member whose account ID sorts first as text, which the provider nominates as the team's Operations admin (see Prerequisites). Have a team admin make it an Operations admin of that team in the Operations UI, or make it a product admin, then apply again: the team is kept and the remaining resources are created. Service accounts are not affected. |
| `403` "not authorized to update maintenance" on a global maintenance window (no `team_id`) | The account running Terraform is not a JSM Operations product admin; being a team's Operations admin is not enough for global maintenance windows. Make it a product admin, or create global maintenance windows with an admin's or a service account's token |
